<p>Thanks.</p>
<p>I don't see a Euclid rating as being necessary simply because so far they aren't actually dangerous, just too weird to allow out into the world. That could change if:</p>
<p>Fossils might weaken the concrete, I hadn't considered that. Instead of including a lengthy Foundation test, I might just add a note in the incident report mentioning that the museum wall collapsed due to structural weakness once the fossils reached a critical concentration. I'll think about it.</p>
<p>I thought about the possibility of eggs or plankton surviving in pulverized debris, but it didn't seem an interesting enough detail to force readers to slog through a description of it. I could amend the containment procedures to include pulverizing rock, then converting it to an acidic slurry which would dissolve any fossilized material it still contained.</p>
<p>Good comments, thanks.</p>

